  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nSurface high pressure remains dominant across the Great Lakes\nregion tonight into Tuesday. Light winds and mainly clear skies\ntonight will facilitate additional fog development (locally\ndense in spots) late tonight into early Tuesday morning as air \ntemperatures drop below crossover values. We have maintained the\ninherited mention of fog through early Tuesday morning and \nexpanded it to include most of the forecast area east of I-39. \nFog will dissipate by mid-morning, giving way to a mild and dry \nautumn day, albeit with increasing mid- and high-level \ncloudiness in the afternoon. Temperatures will again peak in the\n70s to near 80, except slightly cooler along the Illinois Lake \nMichigan shoreline.\n\nWhile quiet weather persists through Tuesday, a wetter pattern \ntakes over mid- to late week. Following tonight's landfall of \nHurricane Polo in Baja California, its remnants will track \nrapidly northeastward into New Mexico before merging with a \nlongwave trough advancing across the Desert Southwest into \nWednesday. At the same time, strengthening southerly low-level \nflow will stream deep Gulf moisture (precipitable water values \nexceeding 1.9 inches) northward toward an approaching cold \nfront. The front will gradually slide southward into the Midwest\nand western Great Lakes Wednesday into Thursday, setting the \nstage for periods of heavy rainfall from the Desert Southwest \ninto the lower Great Lakes.\n\nRainfall chances will ramp up across northwestern Illinois and \nsouthern Wisconsin on Wednesday as a lead wave ejects eastward \nacross the Corn Belt. Uncertainty remains regarding how far \nsoutheast this axis of rainfall will extend during the day, as \nthe surface boundary lags to the northwest. Most areas south of \nI-80 could remain dry through the day, leading to a sharp \ntemperature gradient across the region: highs in the 60s to low \n70s in rainy areas, and near 80 in drier southern areas.\n\nEventually, the frontal zone and main axis of rainfall will \nshift east-southeastward across the entire forecast area on \nThursday as combined Pacific and Gulf moisture streams into the \nGreat Lakes. Total rainfall amounts and the exit speed of the \nrain shield remain uncertain. However, near-record precipitable \nwater values near 2 inches (rare for early October per sounding \nclimatology at ILX and DVN) suggest rainfall could exceed 2 to 3\ninches in localized areas through Thursday night.\n\nThe surface cold front should move south of the area by Friday,\nending the wet pattern as high pressure builds in for the\nweekend. While the forecast maintains low-end rain chances\nFriday afternoon into the weekend, conditions are expected to\nremain largely dry. Cooler temperatures with highs in the 60s\nwill return Friday through the weekend.\n\nKJB",
